mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of short-form video scripting in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nVolatile figures — attribute, mark **verify-quarterly**. The principle (watch-through rules; hook + loop +\nsound-off decide it) is stable; the exact numbers move.\n\n## The metric that rules\n- **Watch-through / completion is the single most important signal.** Platforms test a short on a small audience\n  and expand it based on swipe-away rate, watch-through %, and replay rate (creator/algorithm round-ups, 2026).\n  Shorter often wins because it completes — a 15s short watched fully = 100% retention.\n- **Length sweet spot ~15–35s** for most niches (education can stretch to ~45–60s if every ~5–8s delivers new\n  value); ~75 spoken words ≈ ~30s. Don't stretch a 20s idea to 45s. (attribute; verify-quarterly)\n- **Platform retention benchmarks** (Socialinsider 2025, attribute): TikTok ~78%, YouTube Shorts ~73%, Reels\n  ~65% average view-through — directional, verify-quarterly.\n\n## The first ~3 seconds\n- **~50–60% of drop-off happens in the first 3 seconds** (OpusClip data; attribute). Viewers decide in\n  ~1.5–3s. Cut the intro/logo/\"hey guys\"; lead with the most striking frame + a specific promise.\n- **Layered hooks win.** A hook hitting visual + on-screen text + verbal at once is reported to roughly **3×** the\n  3-second hold vs a single-element intro (2026 analyses; attribute). Captions appear from the first word.\n\n## Sound-off is the default\n- **~80–85% watch on mute** (Zebracat 2025 ~80%; other 2026 figures ~85%; attribute). Write the **muted version\n  first** — on-screen text/captions must carry the whole story; test the cut on mute before publishing.\n- **Burned-in captions lift retention ~15–25%** (OpusClip; attribute) and some algorithms read caption text as a\n  ranking/discovery signal.\n\n## Pacing, payoff & the loop\n- **Visual change every ~2–4s**; never hold one shot past ~3 sentences / ~8s of talk without a change; open a\n  curiosity loop early and pay it off late; cut any second that isn't earning the next.\n- **The payoff must keep the hook's promise.** Over-promising causes a sharp mid-video retention cliff that the\n  algorithm punishes — so an honest hook is a *growth* tactic, not just ethics (\"make a promise, then keep it\").\n- **Loops/replays are weighted heavily** (YouTube confirms it considers replay + looping). Design the last frame\n  to flow back to frame 1 so a re-watch feels intentional; the peak-end rule rewards a strong close.\n\n## House metrics\n3-second hold, average view duration / watch-through %, replays, saves/shares — read natively; never fabricate a\nnumber or guarantee virality.\n"
